#!/bin/bash
# Pipe grep/cat/env output through this to redact secret VALUES before they hit a transcript.
# Usage: grep -rn GITHUB_TOKEN /home/lumen/agent | mask-secrets
#
# Lockstep copy of harness/masking.py PATTERNS (claude-foreman). sed -E is POSIX ERE
# with no lookaround, so masking.py's `(?<![A-Za-z0-9_])` is spelled here as a CAPTURED
# `(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])` that the replacement re-emits — every prefix group is shifted one
# to the right, hence `\1\2MASKED`.
#
# wq#2294/wq#2292: unanchored, `ghs_` matched inside the repo name `highs_solver` and
# the hit is a pri-1 rotate-today alarm, so the false positive cost a whole rotation
# ticket for a credential that never existed. Here it also destroyed evidence: the tool
# instinct 4 tells every agent to pipe through rewrote the plain word `highs_solver` to
# `ghs_MASKED`, which blocked the first pass of the wq#2294 mask-hit census. The
# boundary never hides a real token — a credential is a standalone value, so what
# precedes it is `"`, `:`, `=`, `/`, `-` or space, never an alphanumeric and never `_`.
#
# wq#2400: `_` joined that class. Anchored on alnums alone, `see notes_on_xoxb-<two
# english words>` — from the parametrize list below, drafted for this very bundle — was
# still read as a slack token, which armed the alarm and filed a rotation ticket. A
# prefix after `_` is the tail of an identifier, and a value the identifier LABELS
# (`SLACK_TOKEN=xoxb-…`) is still masked, by the kv-secret rule and by the `=`.
#
# Known divergence from masking.py, owned by wq#2313: the kv-secret rule below keeps
# its keyword list and stays UNanchored (it keys on an English word, not a prefix;
# anchoring it would miss `githubToken: ...`), and it has no tail lookahead, so it
# still fires on `key = apply_key_allowlist(reg)`. That costs this copy a false alarm,
# never a missed mask.
#
# wq#2697/wq#2687/wq#2732 — THE SENTINEL. masking.py solves two problems with one
# per-character negative lookahead (`_body()`, wq#2638): a greedy run may not cross the
# mask literal, and the far side of the literal is a legal place for a value to start.
# POSIX ERE has no lookaround, so this copy spells the same thing structurally: the
# literal is swapped for a byte no rule's charset contains (SOH, `\x01`) before the
# rules run, every rule REPLACES with that byte, and the last line puts the literal
# back. A run then stops at our own output because the charset ends there, not because
# a pattern talked it into stopping early — including at a literal an EARLIER RULE in
# this same pass emitted, which the lookahead also covers and no prefix-side trick can.
#
# What this replaced, and why it had to go (wq#2726 -> wq#2732): the first mirror of
# wq#2638 forced the cfut_/kv-secret body's LAST character to be alnum
# (`{5,}[A-Za-z0-9]`, `{11,}[A-Za-z0-9]`), reasoning that a sentence-final `.` could
# then never be eaten. It cannot distinguish "the run walked past our own literal into
# prose" from "the secret really ends in `.`/`_`/`-`", and POSIX leftmost-longest
# answers both by backing off to the longest prefix ending in alnum. A real value
# ending in punctuation therefore LEAKED that character next to the mask, and one
# sitting exactly at the rule's floor had no legal prefix at all and passed through in
# the clear (`TOKEN: <12 chars ending in _>`). It shipped, and reached bin/ on the
# fleet, because every one of the 30 tests here drew its fixtures from the alnum-tailed
# population the rule was written around — none could have failed (wq#2630).
#
# The sentinel needs no floor adjustment: the quantifiers below are masking.py's own.
# Its one cost is that a literal SOH byte already in the input comes out as `MASKED` —
# over-masking a control byte in a text stream, the direction that never leaks.
#
# wq#2760: masking.py's url-auth rule (wq#2514) was never carried here, so a DSN —
# `DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:<pw>@host/db`, no label this copy knows — printed the
# password in the clear, and burned a freshly rotated one during wq#2700. The `s#...#`
# line masks what sits between `scheme://user:` and `@host` for any scheme; the user
# may be empty (`redis://:<pw>@host`) and the password may hold a raw `@` (the run
# backtracks to the last `@host` before a `/` or space, as DSN parsers read it).
# Deliberately WIDER than masking.py, which can afford precision because a hit there is
# a pri-1 alarm: no body floor and none of its placeholder/env-ref exclusions, so a
# short or templated password is masked here too. Over-masking a pointer is free; a
# 7-char DSN password printed is burned. `\b` is masking.py's `_WB`: the sentinel is
# not a word character, so the far side of our own literal is already a boundary.
#
# wq#2720: a wide body can swallow a DIFFERENT rule's fixed prefix whole, not just run
# into it. `ghp_<body>AKIA<16>xoxb-<body>` (wq#2702/wq#2705's verifier, glued with no
# separator) ran github's `[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}` straight through `AKIA` and the letters
# `xoxb`, stopping only at the `-` that charset can't cross — the slack rule never saw
# its `xoxb-` prefix, and its body printed in the clear. Same shape for any rule whose
# charset covers another's prefix letters: cloudflare's and kv-secret's `[..._.-]`
# cover all five other prefixes whole, sk-key's and slack's dashed charsets cover most
# of them.
#
# POSIX ERE has no lookahead, so a body can't be told "stop before AKIA" the way
# masking.py's `_body()` does. Instead, before each rule below runs, every OTHER
# rule's fixed prefix is marked with a byte no charset contains (STX, `\x02`) —
# `s/(other prefixes)/\x02&/g` — so a greedy run halts at the mark instead of reading
# through it, and the mark is stripped again immediately after, before the next rule
# reads the text. Stripping it back out (rather than leaving it) is what keeps the
# wq#2292/wq#2400 false-positive fixes intact: `ghs_` inside `highs_solver` or `xoxb-`
# inside `notes_on_xoxb-` never gets an artificial boundary from a mark left lying
# around, because by the time THAT prefix's own rule runs, the mark around it has
# already been removed and the true, unmasked character is back in front of it. A
# rule's OWN prefix is never marked against itself: `cfut_<live>cfut_MASKED`
# (wq#2638) must still compose as one rule reading its own repeated prefix, not stop
# short of it. The kv-secret line deliberately gets NO bracket: its whole point is to
# catch a labelled value that IS another rule's shape (`TOKEN: sk-ant-<token>`,
# already masked by the sk-key line above it), and a mark at position zero would
# refuse that value outright, floor and all, for anything the earlier rule's OWN
# stricter body missed (`TOKEN: AKIA` followed by lowercase junk matches no AWS key
# shape, but is still an opaque value behind a label) — an actual leak, not a
# false-positive trade.
#
# One bracket-and-strip cycle only fixes the SWALLOW. The rules still run in one
# fixed left-to-right order, and slack (before aws in that order) cannot yet see the
# `MASKED` boundary aws's masking is about to create for it — aws masks `AKIA` and
# only THEN is the character in front of `xoxb-` a legal boundary, one rule too late
# for a single pass. No static reordering satisfies both glue directions (`AKIA` then
# `xoxb-`, and the reverse both occur), so the whole program below runs several times
# over, exactly as masking.py's mask_text() now loops over PATTERNS (wq#2720): each
# already-masked span re-enters as `\x01` on the next pass (line 1) and stays inert
# (the body charset excludes `\x01` too, so a rule never re-matches its own prior
# output), while a rule freed up by THAT pass's masking gets its own turn on the next
# one. Six passes bound the longest possible chain (one per fixed prefix family); a
# stable input converges long before that and every later pass is then a no-op.
#
# wq#2848: the mark, above, was placed on a bare PREFIX substring, not on a value that
# could actually BE the foreign rule's token — four or five ordinary characters, not a
# credential. A github-shaped body carrying a bare `AKIA`/`cfut` substring (no real
# AWS/cloudflare token there, just noise that happens to spell the prefix) got marked
# anyway, the mark truncated the run short of THIS rule's own floor (30/20/16/8), and
# below the floor the whole `{N,}` match fails outright — not a narrower mask, no match
# at ALL, so the real token, fixed prefix included, passes through with no `\x01` and
# no strip to catch it. Measured live: a synthetic github body carrying `AKIA` or
# `cfut` at offset 5/12/20/28 leaked whole, 200/200 trials each (verify-pass wq#2803,
# masking.py's `_foreign_prefixes` docstring). Fixed the same way in both copies: the
# mark below is now the OTHER rule's full shape — its own prefix AND its own
# floor-quantified body — not the bare prefix, so noise that merely looks like a
# prefix is never marked at all and reads through as ordinary body text.
#
# That alone is not sufficient: cloudflare's body charset (`[A-Za-z0-9_.-]`) is a
# superset of plain alnum, so a github body carrying a bare `cfut` substring followed
# by 6+ ordinary characters genuinely satisfies cloudflare's FULL shape too, and would
# still mark early. So each of the five open-floor rules below also admits the mark
# byte (`\x02`) into its OWN charset for exactly the first `{N}` characters of the run
# — a mark cannot even be considered until this rule's own floor is already behind it
# — and only excludes `\x02` (so a mark still halts the run) in the unbounded `*` tail
# that follows. `AKIA`'s own rule needs neither change: its body charset (`[0-9A-Z]`)
# cannot spell any of the other five prefixes at all (each contains a lowercase
# letter, `_` or `-`), so a mark placed against it was already inert.
#
# wq#2899: wq#2848's mark-the-full-shape fix (above) only helps within ONE rule's
# OWN traversal — the five rules still run in a fixed order, so by the time a
# LATER rule's turn comes, an EARLIER rule may already have masked its own token,
# leaving `<its kept prefix>\x01` sitting there instead of the live body wq#2848's
# widened `{N}` window needs. `\x01` is in no charset, so the run still stops dead
# below its own floor, and the whole match still fails — a real live gap this
# short, right in front of another rule's already-masked residue, is what wq#2848's
# own `RULES` comment in harness/tests/test_mask_foreign_prefix_matrix.py named as
# a known, tracked residual (an sk- token whose body embeds `cfut_`/`cfut` or leads
# with `xoxb-`; cloudflare and slack sit on either side of sk-key in this file's
# rule order, so both glue directions occur). One absorb line per open-floor rule,
# run right after its own strip, closes it: boundary + this rule's own prefix +
# ANY LENGTH of its own charset + one of the OTHER rules' bare kept prefix +
# `\x01` collapses to boundary + prefix + `\x01` — no mark byte needed here, since
# the trailing part is a literal a `*` gives characters back to satisfy (the same
# greedy-then-required-suffix shape POSIX ERE already resolves correctly for the
# boundary alternation itself). The trailing list excludes the rule's OWN prefix,
# same as the mark line above it and for the same reason (wq#2638): a value like
# `sk-ant-<live>sk-MASKED` must stay a live alarm on the `<live>` half, not
# collapse into the kept prefix the way a genuinely foreign rule's residue does.
# That specific shape — a letters-only run immediately in front of a SECOND
# `sk-` — is a separate, pre-existing quirk of the greedy `sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*`
# prefix (it can absorb the gap into ITS OWN kept prefix before this line ever
# runs) and is out of scope here; masking.py's mirror docstring
# (`_floored_absorb`) and the matrix test's `_SK_LETTER_EXCLUDES_OWN_PREFIX`
# record the same boundary.
#
# wq#2922: the `\x01` sentinel (line 1 below) is this copy's version of
# masking.py's unconditional MASK-literal guard — no rule's charset admits
# `\x01`, so a body run stops there regardless of floor, exactly like `_body()`'s
# mask_guard. A real value whose body happens to contain the literal `MASKED`
# below its rule's floor therefore still fails the whole match here too, and
# because this script's only output channel IS the rewritten text, going silent
# means the credential reaches stdout whole. Each rule below gets one backstop
# line, inserted between its own floor-satisfying match and the `\x02` strip
# that follows it: same prefix and charset as the rule's own line, admitting
# `\x01` (never `\x02` — see below) as an ordinary body character, open-ended
# `{floor,}` rather than the rule's own exact-then-continuation shape, since
# there is no replacement left to bound: a value that reaches the floor this
# way is replaced with its kept prefix plus `WARN` instead of the credential
# body, whole.
#
# `\x02` is deliberately left OUT of this line's charset, unlike the rule's own
# line above it. A first cut admitted it the same way, reasoning that a
# `\x02`-marked foreign shape within the floor should count toward reachability
# the same way wq#2848 lets it count for the real match — but that composition
# is not this ticket's bug (probe section B never marks a foreign shape; it
# only ever injects the literal), and admitting `\x02` here besides let this
# line re-match text the rule's OWN line just finished masking: `xoxb-` +
# already-replaced `\x01` + a `\x02`-marked, not-yet-processed `AKIA<real key>`
# reads, to a charset admitting BOTH markers, as one 20-character-plus run
# starting right at the kept `xoxb-` prefix — so this line fired AGAIN on the
# rule's own successful output, one pass after masking it, and swallowed the
# separate AWS token beside it into a single `xoxb-WARN`
# (test_mask_secrets_boundary.py::test_cloudflare_residue_does_not_swallow_the_
# seam). Without `\x02` in the charset, that same text stops after one `\x01`
# atom — one character, nowhere near any rule's floor — so it no longer
# matches at all, and the genuinely foreign token beside it is left for its
# own rule's mark-then-match lines to find on the pass they run.
#
# Dropping `\x02` closed that seam but not its sibling: a SHORT foreign
# residue — another rule's kept prefix plus its own `\x01`, `sk-ant-\x01`, too
# short to satisfy sk-key's own floor and so never `\x02`-marked at all (`\x02`
# only ever marks a FULL floor-satisfying shape) — sits in a charset this
# rule's own body admits (letters and a dash are legal cloudflare body
# characters too), so a value like `cfut_` + `MASKEDsk-ant-<live>` — this
# module's own `MASKED` glued directly in front of a DIFFERENT rule's live
# token, a shape wq#2632's doctrine corpus and this file's own `_GLUED`
# fixtures both carry (split the same way there) — read as one `\x01` atom,
# then ran straight through `sk-ant-` and its own now-orphaned `abc...` body,
# swallowing a still-live secret into one kept prefix plus `WARN` (same test
# file, same fixture class, `sk-key` id). One more
# mark-and-strip cycle closes it: immediately before each backstop line, every
# OTHER rule's BARE prefix (no floor requirement, unlike the `\x02` line above
# it) is marked with `\x03`, which this line's charset also never admits — so
# it stops equally at a foreign rule's un-processed live prefix and at its own
# already-processed residue, leaving both for whatever already handles them
# (the foreign rule's own lines, later in this same pass, or nothing further
# at all if it is genuinely spent). `\x02` and `\x03` are stripped together,
# right after the backstop line, before the absorb line below reads `\x01`.
# `WARN` is 4 letters — under cloudflare's floor (the smallest, 6) even in its
# worst case, where `cfut_?`'s optional underscore backtracks OUT of the kept
# prefix and into the body, handing the body one extra character for free (a
# first cut of this line used 5, `ALARM`, and that one borrowed underscore was
# enough to hit cloudflare's floor exactly on the very next of the six passes,
# re-triggering on its own output: `cfut_` + `ALARM` read as prefix `cfut`
# (backtracked, no underscore) + body `_ALARM`, six characters, a live-looking
# value again) — so on the next pass
# it cannot re-satisfy any rule's own backstop and re-fire. It is also not a
# substring of any prefix this file marks, so it is never swept up by another
# rule's `\x02` marking either. A genuine own-residue value (`cfut_\x01`,
# `cfut_\x01.`, `cfut\x01_`) never reaches this line's floor at all: the
# literal collapsed to ONE `\x01` byte, so even cloudflare's floor of 6 needs
# five more real characters behind it, which is exactly what test_masking.
# test_token_engine_mirror_agrees checks no own-output shape ever supplies
# here. No separate hit record is possible in a pure text filter — `WARN` in
# the output is this copy's whole alarm channel, which is why instinct 4
# already tells every agent piping through this tool to treat ANY unexpected
# text in its output as reason to stop and look.
_PROG='
s/MASKED/\x01/g
s/github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|cfut_?[A-Za-z0-9_.-]{6,}|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|xox[bpasr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9\x02]{30}[A-Za-z0-9]*/\1\2\x01/g
s/github_pat_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA/\x03&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9\x01]{30,}/\1\2WARN/g
s/[\x02\x03]//g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]*(github_pat_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA)\x01/\1\2\x01/g
s/(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|cfut_?[A-Za-z0-9_.-]{6,}|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|xox[bpasr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(github_pat_)[A-Za-z0-9_\x02]{20}[A-Za-z0-9_]*/\1\2\x01/g
s/ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA/\x03&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(github_pat_)[A-Za-z0-9_\x01]{20,}/\1\2WARN/g
s/[\x02\x03]//g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(github_pat_)[A-Za-z0-9_]*(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA)\x01/\1\2\x01/g
s/(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|xox[bpasr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(cfut_?)[A-Za-z0-9_.\x02-]{6}[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*/\1\2\x01/g
s/ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA/\x03&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(cfut_?)[A-Za-z0-9_.\x01-]{6,}/\1\2WARN/g
s/[\x02\x03]//g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(cfut_?)[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA)\x01/\1\2\x01/g
s/(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|cfut_?[A-Za-z0-9_.-]{6,}|xox[bpasr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*)[A-Za-z0-9_\x02-]{16}[A-Za-z0-9_-]*/\1\2\x01/g
s/ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|cfut_?|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA/\x03&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*)[A-Za-z0-9_\x01-]{16,}/\1\2WARN/g
s/[\x02\x03]//g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*)[A-Za-z0-9_-]*(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|cfut_?|xox[bpasr]-|AKIA)\x01/\1\2\x01/g
s/(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|cfut_?[A-Za-z0-9_.-]{6,}|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(xox[bpasr]-)[A-Za-z0-9\x02-]{8}[A-Za-z0-9-]*/\1\2\x01/g
s/ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|AKIA/\x03&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(xox[bpasr]-)[A-Za-z0-9\x01-]{8,}/\1\2WARN/g
s/[\x02\x03]//g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(xox[bpasr]-)[A-Za-z0-9-]*(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_|github_pat_|cfut_?|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*|AKIA)\x01/\1\2\x01/g
s/(ghp_|gho_|ghu_|ghs_|ghr_)[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|cfut_?[A-Za-z0-9_.-]{6,}|sk-([a-zA-Z]+-)*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|xox[bpasr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,}/\x02&/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(AKIA)[0-9A-Z]{16}/\1\2\x01/g
s/(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(AKIA)[0-9A-Z\x01]{16,}/\1\2WARN/g
